S|             INT./EXT. TRAIN, ARDSLEY-ON-HUDSON, NEW YORK - MORNING                 
O|
N|             We hear a train’s mournful clatter as trees flash by and then          
N|             the train itself passes in a silvery streak.                           
O|
N|             A woman draws an “x” on her foggy window-- looking out                 
N,G|             through it directly at us:                                             
O|
N,I|             This is RACHEL and she is THE GIRL ON THE TRAIN.                       
O|
C,E|                                 RACHEL (SOFT V.O.)                                 
U|                       ...My husband used to tell me I                              
U|                       have an overactive imagination. I                            
U|                       can’t help it. I mean, haven’t you                           
U|                       ever been on a train and wondered                            
U|                       about the lives of the people who                            
U|                       live near the tracks? The lives                              
U|                       you’ve never lived.                                          
N,G|             RACHEL’S POV: Out the window: A series of backyards slowly             
N|             passes:                                                                
O|
N|             A MAN walks towards his front yard.                                    
O|
N|             An OLDER MAN works on a vintage car in his yard.                       
N|             CHILDREN slide down playground equipment.                              
O|
N|             A LONE BOY throws a ball. His dog chases after it.                     
O|
T|                                                                  CUT TO:           
Y|                                     Title: RACHEL                                  
O|
